{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LjV4T4qfvZt7JHj3gbGPD3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `r2_1_interactive_no` test fails: our binary exits 0 but gmv exits 1 when the user says \"no\" to an interactive prompt. Let me check what GNU mv's actual exit code is when -i is used and the user says \"no\".\n\nActually, this is interesting. GNU mv exits 1 when the user declines to overwrite with -i? Let me think about this...\n\nActually, looking at the GNU coreutils source code, when the user says \"no\" to an interactive prompt, mv does NOT move the file. But what exit code does it return?\n\nLooking at the error output more carefully, the test says:\n\n\nexit code differs: go=0 ref=1\n\nSo gmv exits 1 when the user says \"no\" to an interactive overwrite prompt. I need to match that behavior—when the user declines an interactive overwrite, we should exit 1, not 0.\n\nBut I should double-check the no-clobber case: gmv exits 0 when skipping with `-n`, which our tests already pass. The difference is that interactive rejection should be treated as a failure, while silent skipping with `-n` is not.\n\nI need to distinguish between these two cases in the code. The cleanest approach is to have `checkOverwrite` return an action type that can represent three states: proceed normally, skip without error, or skip with an error code. Then `moveSingle` and `moveMultiple` can handle each case appropriately.\n\nDefining the action type... Separating the skip behaviors cleanly. For the batch operation with interactive mode, if the user declines any file, the exit code should be 1 to match gmv's behavior, so I need to set `code = 1` whenever a file is skipped due to user rejection.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":857,"cache_read_input_tokens":72800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemera